---
import PolicyLayout from '../layouts/PolicyLayout.astro';
---

<PolicyLayout
  title="Therapeutic agreement"
  description="Therapeutic agreement and expectations for process-based music therapy with That Music Therapy Lot — roles, boundaries, and how we work together."
>
  <p class="policy-intro">
    This agreement sets clear frame boundaries, roles, and expectations for the
    therapeutic relationship. Starting process-based music therapy with Ben Richardson
    (That Music Therapy Lot) means we each commit to the points below. You will also be
    asked to confirm you have read and agree to this agreement at the start of work (for
    example in a first session or via an intake form).
  </p>

  <h2>1. What the therapist agrees to</h2>

  <h3>Time &amp; attendance</h3>
  <p>Attend sessions promptly on the agreed day and time.</p>

  <h3>Notice of changes</h3>
  <p>
    Provide clear advance notice when sessions are coming to a natural pause or end
    (for planned breaks, holidays, or block completion), or if a session needs to be
    rescheduled or cancelled.
  </p>

  <h3>Illness</h3>
  <p>
    Inform you of any sudden illness or inability to attend as soon as reasonably possible.
  </p>

  <h3>Availability</h3>
  <p>
    Be fully present and available to you during session times, and respond to
    administrative or logistically necessary queries outside of session times within
    reasonable working hours.
  </p>

  <h3>Transparency &amp; safeguarding</h3>
  <p>
    Always communicate clearly if something shared in a session needs to be disclosed
    externally due to a safeguarding concern or a risk to your safety or the safety of others.
  </p>

  <h3>Technical contingencies</h3>
  <p>
    If an online session call drops due to connection issues, follow up immediately via
    telephone to reconnect or safely conclude the session.
  </p>

  <h3>Professional accountability</h3>
  <p>
    Maintain active registration with the Health and Care Professions Council (HCPC) as a
    Music Therapist, adhere to HCPC standards of proficiency and conduct, and provide
    registration details upon request so you know how to raise concerns regarding fitness
    to practice. Concerns about fitness to practice may be raised with the
    {' '}<a href="https://www.hcpc-uk.org/concerns/raising-concerns/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">HCPC</a>
    {' '}and, where relevant, via the
    {' '}<a href="https://www.bamt.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">British Association for Music Therapy (BAMT)</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Unconditional regard</h3>
  <p>
    Always regard you positively, working from the foundation that you are doing the
    best you can.
  </p>

  <h2>2. What you (the client) agree to</h2>

  <h3>Attendance</h3>
  <p>Attend sessions on time, at the agreed-upon day and time.</p>

  <h3>Notice &amp; absence</h3>
  <p>
    Provide as much notice as possible if you are unable to attend a session, and notify
    the therapist of sudden illness as early as possible. Cancellation and fee terms are
    set out in the <a href="/payment-policy">Payment &amp; cancellation policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Approach</h3>
  <p>
    Attend sessions with an open mind and a curiosity about what is possible in your process.
  </p>

  <h3>Communication boundaries</h3>
  <p>
    Ensure that contacting the therapist outside of session times (for example via email
    or messaging) does not replace or substitute the therapeutic work itself. This includes avoiding:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Frequent or high-volume messaging between sessions.</li>
    <li>Expecting instant or immediate responses outside of scheduled times.</li>
    <li>Engaging primarily over email rather than attending agreed session times.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Communication in session</h3>
  <p>
    Communicate if you are finding a session difficult, distressing, or overwhelming, so
    that a sudden departure (for example closing a laptop or leaving the room) is not
    mistaken for a dropped internet connection or a sudden acute medical emergency.
  </p>

  <h3>Mutual respect</h3>
  <p>
    Assume that the therapist regards you positively and that questions asked during
    sessions stem from a place of therapeutic exploration, not judgment.
  </p>

  <h2>Related policies</h2>
  <p>
    Please also read the
    {' '}<a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy policy</a>,
    {' '}<a href="/payment-policy">Payment &amp; cancellation policy</a>, and
    {' '}<a href="/safeguarding-and-complaints">Safeguarding &amp; complaints policy</a>.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
